{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your client can call your server code directly, so you do not want to write an HTTP route for chat. Hand that function to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":". Pick the helper from what your function returns:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your function"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"},{"type":"text","value":" synchronously (in-process "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":", an RSC stream, tests)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#in-process-streams","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":", such as a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" server function"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#async-functions-with-fetcher","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is an RPC method that returns an async iterable"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#rpc-clients-with-rpcstream","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rpcStream()"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The choice depends on whether your function is sync or async. Both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":" can yield an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"},{"type":"text","value":". A server function returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":", so it does not type-check in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":". All three become the same request-scoped adapter. So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":", abort, errors, and tool calls work the same."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"in-process-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In-process streams"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" takes a factory that returns an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"},{"type":"text","value":" synchronously. It connects that iterable straight to the client:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, stream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatServerFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\n// `chatServerFn` is an in-process server-side function that synchronously\n// returns an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>, for example the result of\n// `chat()` on the server.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: stream((messages, data) => chatServerFn({ messages, ...data })),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory gets the conversation messages and the per-request "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage"},{"type":"text","value":". Return any async iterable that yields "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" objects: a generator, the output of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":", or a transformed stream."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" is request-scoped. For each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage"},{"type":"text","value":", it calls the factory once, runs the iterable to its end, and closes the connection. For one long-lived channel that carries many sends, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./custom#persistent-transports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Transports"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers. These let server-driven persistence ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: true"},{"type":"text","value":") work without an HTTP endpoint. Each one is usually a one-line call into your server:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrate"},{"type":"text","value":": restores a chat thread."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrateGeneration"},{"type":"text","value":": restores the last run of a generation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":": replays a run that is still in flight."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the full wiring, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation Persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"async-functions-with-fetcher","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async functions with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" server function always returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass it as the top-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":" option, not as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection"},{"type":"text","value":". Give "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" exactly one of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection"},{"type":"text","value":". The option works like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":" on the "},{"type":"link","href":"../media/generation-hooks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generation hooks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most common server function ends with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" and resolves to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// server/chat.server.ts\nimport { createServerFn } from \"@tanstack/react-start\";\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport const chatFn = createServerFn({ method: \"POST\" })\n  .inputValidator((data: { messages: Array<UIMessage> }) => data)\n  .handler(({ data }) =>\n    toServerSentEventsResponse(\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages: data.messages }),\n    ),\n  );","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  fetcher: ({ messages }, { signal }) => chatFn({ data: { messages }, signal }),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fetcher gets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ messages, data, threadId, runId }"},{"type":"text","value":" and an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":". The signal fires on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":", or when a new send replaces the active one. Return one of these:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the chat client parses its SSE body for you."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the client reads it directly. Use this when your server function returns the stream itself, not a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fetcher can return the value directly or in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The generation hooks ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateImage"},{"type":"text","value":" and the others) also accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrateGeneration"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" options next to their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":". So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: true"},{"type":"text","value":" hydrates and rejoins through server functions, with no HTTP route. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation Persistence: Server functions / direct"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rpc-clients-with-rpcstream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RPC clients with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rpcStream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rpcStream()"},{"type":"text","value":" works the same as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":". The name reads better when you call an RPC client. Use it with Cap'n Web, gRPC-Web, tRPC subscriptions, or any RPC framework that returns an async iterable:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, rpcStream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { api } from \"./rpc-client\";\n\n// `api.chat.stream` is your RPC method. It must return an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: rpcStream((messages, data) =>\n    api.chat.stream({ messages, ...data }),\n  ),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rpcStream()"},{"type":"text","value":" takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ hydrate, hydrateGeneration, joinRun }"},{"type":"text","value":"). Then server-driven persistence works over RPC. Each handler is usually a one-line RPC call."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage(\"Hello\")"},{"type":"text","value":". The reply streams into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":", and your app has no chat route of its own."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"in-process-streams","text":"In-process streams","level":2},{"id":"async-functions-with-fetcher","text":"Async functions with fetcher","level":2},{"id":"rpc-clients-with-rpcstream","text":"RPC clients with rpcStream","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYour client can call your server code directly, so you do not want to write an HTTP route for chat. Hand that function to `useChat`. Pick the helper from what your function returns:\n\n| Your function | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| Returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` synchronously (in-process `chat()`, an RSC stream, tests) | [`stream()`](#in-process-streams) |\n| Returns a `Promise`, such as a [TanStack Start](https://tanstack.com/start) server function | [`fetcher`](#async-functions-with-fetcher) |\n| Is an RPC method that returns an async iterable | [`rpcStream()`](#rpc-clients-with-rpcstream) |\n\nThe choice depends on whether your function is sync or async. Both `stream()` and `fetcher` can yield an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`. A server function returns a `Promise`, so it does not type-check in `stream()`. All three become the same request-scoped adapter. So `stop()`, abort, errors, and tool calls work the same.\n\n## In-process streams\n\n`stream()` takes a factory that returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` synchronously. It connects that iterable straight to the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, stream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatServerFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\n// `chatServerFn` is an in-process server-side function that synchronously\n// returns an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>, for example the result of\n// `chat()` on the server.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: stream((messages, data) => chatServerFn({ messages, ...data })),\n});\n```\n\nThe factory gets the conversation messages and the per-request `data` that you passed to `sendMessage`. Return any async iterable that yields `StreamChunk` objects: a generator, the output of `chat()`, or a transformed stream.\n\n`stream()` is request-scoped. For each `sendMessage`, it calls the factory once, runs the iterable to its end, and closes the connection. For one long-lived channel that carries many sends, see [Custom Transports](./custom#persistent-transports).\n\n`stream()` takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers. These let server-driven persistence (`persistence: true`) work without an HTTP endpoint. Each one is usually a one-line call into your server:\n\n- `hydrate`: restores a chat thread.\n- `hydrateGeneration`: restores the last run of a generation.\n- `joinRun`: replays a run that is still in flight.\n\nFor the full wiring, see [Generation Persistence](../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct).\n\n## Async functions with `fetcher`\n\nA [TanStack Start](https://tanstack.com/start) server function always returns a `Promise`. Pass it as the top-level `fetcher` option, not as a `connection`. Give `useChat` exactly one of `fetcher` or `connection`. The option works like `fetcher` on the [generation hooks](../media/generation-hooks).\n\nThe most common server function ends with `toServerSentEventsResponse(...)` and resolves to a `Response`:\n\n```typescript ignore\n// server/chat.server.ts\nimport { createServerFn } from \"@tanstack/react-start\";\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport const chatFn = createServerFn({ method: \"POST\" })\n  .inputValidator((data: { messages: Array<UIMessage> }) => data)\n  .handler(({ data }) =>\n    toServerSentEventsResponse(\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages: data.messages }),\n    ),\n  );\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  fetcher: ({ messages }, { signal }) => chatFn({ data: { messages }, signal }),\n});\n```\n\nThe fetcher gets `{ messages, data, threadId, runId }` and an `AbortSignal`. The signal fires on `stop()`, or when a new send replaces the active one. Return one of these:\n\n- **A `Response`**: the chat client parses its SSE body for you.\n- **An `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`**: the client reads it directly. Use this when your server function returns the stream itself, not a `Response`.\n\nThe fetcher can return the value directly or in a `Promise`.\n\n> **Tip:** The generation hooks (`useGenerateImage` and the others) also accept `hydrateGeneration` and `joinRun` options next to their `fetcher`. So `persistence: true` hydrates and rejoins through server functions, with no HTTP route. See [Generation Persistence: Server functions / direct](../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct).\n\n## RPC clients with `rpcStream`\n\n`rpcStream()` works the same as `stream()`. The name reads better when you call an RPC client. Use it with Cap'n Web, gRPC-Web, tRPC subscriptions, or any RPC framework that returns an async iterable:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, rpcStream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { api } from \"./rpc-client\";\n\n// `api.chat.stream` is your RPC method. It must return an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: rpcStream((messages, data) =>\n    api.chat.stream({ messages, ...data }),\n  ),\n});\n```\n\nLike `stream()`, `rpcStream()` takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers (`{ hydrate, hydrateGeneration, joinRun }`). Then server-driven persistence works over RPC. Each handler is usually a one-line RPC call.\n\nCall `sendMessage(\"Hello\")`. The reply streams into `messages`, and your app has no chat route of its own.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Server Functions\nid: transports-server-functions\norder: 6\ndescription: \"Call your server code directly from useChat with stream(), fetcher, or rpcStream(): TanStack Start server functions, RSC streams, RPC clients, and tests.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - server functions\n  - tanstack start\n  - fetcher\n  - stream\n  - rpcStream\n  - rpc\n  - async iterable\n---\n\nYour client can call your server code directly, so you do not want to write an HTTP route for chat. Hand that function to `useChat`. Pick the helper from what your function returns:\n\n| Your function | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| Returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` synchronously (in-process `chat()`, an RSC stream, tests) | [`stream()`](#in-process-streams) |\n| Returns a `Promise`, such as a [TanStack Start](https://tanstack.com/start) server function | [`fetcher`](#async-functions-with-fetcher) |\n| Is an RPC method that returns an async iterable | [`rpcStream()`](#rpc-clients-with-rpcstream) |\n\nThe choice depends on whether your function is sync or async. Both `stream()` and `fetcher` can yield an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`. A server function returns a `Promise`, so it does not type-check in `stream()`. All three become the same request-scoped adapter. So `stop()`, abort, errors, and tool calls work the same.\n\n## In-process streams\n\n`stream()` takes a factory that returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` synchronously. It connects that iterable straight to the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, stream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatServerFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\n// `chatServerFn` is an in-process server-side function that synchronously\n// returns an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>, for example the result of\n// `chat()` on the server.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: stream((messages, data) => chatServerFn({ messages, ...data })),\n});\n```\n\nThe factory gets the conversation messages and the per-request `data` that you passed to `sendMessage`. Return any async iterable that yields `StreamChunk` objects: a generator, the output of `chat()`, or a transformed stream.\n\n`stream()` is request-scoped. For each `sendMessage`, it calls the factory once, runs the iterable to its end, and closes the connection. For one long-lived channel that carries many sends, see [Custom Transports](./custom#persistent-transports).\n\n`stream()` takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers. These let server-driven persistence (`persistence: true`) work without an HTTP endpoint. Each one is usually a one-line call into your server:\n\n- `hydrate`: restores a chat thread.\n- `hydrateGeneration`: restores the last run of a generation.\n- `joinRun`: replays a run that is still in flight.\n\nFor the full wiring, see [Generation Persistence](../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct).\n\n## Async functions with `fetcher`\n\nA [TanStack Start](https://tanstack.com/start) server function always returns a `Promise`. Pass it as the top-level `fetcher` option, not as a `connection`. Give `useChat` exactly one of `fetcher` or `connection`. The option works like `fetcher` on the [generation hooks](../media/generation-hooks).\n\nThe most common server function ends with `toServerSentEventsResponse(...)` and resolves to a `Response`:\n\n```typescript ignore\n// server/chat.server.ts\nimport { createServerFn } from \"@tanstack/react-start\";\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport const chatFn = createServerFn({ method: \"POST\" })\n  .inputValidator((data: { messages: Array<UIMessage> }) => data)\n  .handler(({ data }) =>\n    toServerSentEventsResponse(\n      chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages: data.messages }),\n    ),\n  );\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { chatFn } from \"./server/chat.server\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  fetcher: ({ messages }, { signal }) => chatFn({ data: { messages }, signal }),\n});\n```\n\nThe fetcher gets `{ messages, data, threadId, runId }` and an `AbortSignal`. The signal fires on `stop()`, or when a new send replaces the active one. Return one of these:\n\n- **A `Response`**: the chat client parses its SSE body for you.\n- **An `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`**: the client reads it directly. Use this when your server function returns the stream itself, not a `Response`.\n\nThe fetcher can return the value directly or in a `Promise`.\n\n> **Tip:** The generation hooks (`useGenerateImage` and the others) also accept `hydrateGeneration` and `joinRun` options next to their `fetcher`. So `persistence: true` hydrates and rejoins through server functions, with no HTTP route. See [Generation Persistence: Server functions / direct](../persistence/generation-persistence#server-functions--direct).\n\n## RPC clients with `rpcStream`\n\n`rpcStream()` works the same as `stream()`. The name reads better when you call an RPC client. Use it with Cap'n Web, gRPC-Web, tRPC subscriptions, or any RPC framework that returns an async iterable:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, rpcStream } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { api } from \"./rpc-client\";\n\n// `api.chat.stream` is your RPC method. It must return an AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>.\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: rpcStream((messages, data) =>\n    api.chat.stream({ messages, ...data }),\n  ),\n});\n```\n\nLike `stream()`, `rpcStream()` takes an optional second argument with persistence handlers (`{ hydrate, hydrateGeneration, joinRun }`). Then server-driven persistence works over RPC. Each handler is usually a one-line RPC call.\n\nCall `sendMessage(\"Hello\")`. The reply streams into `messages`, and your app has no chat route of its own.\n"}
